## Testing and review

- Check the main changed behavior and realistic failures that matter. Add focused
  regression tests for meaningful bugs when practical. Small cosmetic, prose, and
  straightforward configuration changes may need no new tests.
- No coverage targets, exhaustive edge-case matrices, trivial tests, or new testing
  infrastructure for small tasks. Reuse existing fixtures and valid passing results.
- Start with focused checks. Broaden for concrete risk or explicit essential gates;
  do not rerun everything after cosmetic edits. Never hide failures or claim unrun
  checks passed. Unrelated failures and unavailable optional checks are notes.
- Review substantial or higher-risk changes; small routine changes need no independent
  reviewer. Block only concrete important acceptance failures, material regressions,
  or credible serious security/data-integrity defects introduced by the change.
- Optional coverage, minor defects, polish, speculative hardening, and unrelated
  findings do not block delivery. One review plus a focused repair/re-review if needed.
- For changed access controls or destructive data operations, verify the relevant
  denied/failure behavior. No generic repository-wide audit per task.
- Update existing documentation only when useful instructions/contracts changed.

## Execution and Git

- Tech-lead owns planning; overnight coordinates runner-assigned tasks. Implementation
  agents do not commit or update task state. Preserve pre-existing work.
- Existing long backlogs/title-only tasks remain valid. Adapt instructions in place,
  preserve product acceptance and outcomes, and resume useful partial implementation.
- Approved scope permits reasonable implementation decisions and routine repairs.
  Ask only for consequential scope decisions, essential missing capabilities, or
  actions outside these limits. A request to fix a blocked task authorizes recovery
  and local task commits; no extra formal recovery approval process.
- Runner: bash ./run-tonight.sh from the project root; requires an existing Git repo,
  planning files, and scripts/backlog.py. One runner per checkout.
- Completed tasks get verified commits. Commit authorization: runner assignments and
  owner-requested recovery authorize local task commits. Style: <COMMIT_CONVENTION>.
- Git workflow: <PROJECT_RULES_OR_INSTALLED_WORKFLOW>.
  For sprint work, replace the preceding line with this exact selection:

Use the installed `sprint-workflow` skill for branching and pull requests.

- Remove the selection line if unused. Human-only merges; no force-push, history
  rewriting, hook skipping, deployment, or discarding user changes.
- Runner continues through eligible independent tasks. Sprint runs use one task per
  session and return to the fetched sprint after clean delivered tasks; dependent
  tasks wait for human merges. Dirty partial task work is preserved and stops branch
  transitions until recovered. Non-sprint sessions default to three assigned tasks.
- Default three substantive repairs per task, excluding initial implementation and
  mechanical corrections. Automatic resumption preserves attempts; an explicit owner
  recovery request may renew the repair budget. Fixable unfinished work resumes by
  default; real blockers become [!] while independent work continues when safe.

## Completion and communication

Done means agreed behavior works, focused/essential checks pass, any important review
blockers are resolved, and authorized delivery is verified. [x] records delivery,
not merge/deployment. Do not add requirements merely to certify perfection.

Keep updates short and practical: usually five bullets or fewer. Explain issues before
task IDs; no internal-process jargon. BACKLOG.md holds state, .opencode/progress.md
holds compact recovery checkpoints, and DONE.md gets one brief session summary.
Ignore progress/log files in Git. Do not produce duplicate reports or session diaries.
